@page "/"

<MudContainer MaxWidth="MaxWidth.Medium">

<PageTitle>Home</PageTitle>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h3" Class="mt-4" GutterBottom="true">Disaster Impact Estimation Using Social Media</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h6" GutterBottom="true">A Scalable Framework for Rapid Disaster Assessment and Visualization</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
    Natural disasters such as hurricanes and tropical storms cause widespread damage, requiring rapid and 
    accurate assessment to support emergency response and recovery. Traditional Preliminary Damage 
    Assessments (PDA) rely on field surveys, insurance claims, and self-reporting, which are often time-intensive and resource-constrained.
</MudText>

<MudPaper Class="p-4 my-6">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Align="Align.Center" Class="mb-2">Impact of Disasters</MudText>

    <!-- Wrapper gives the bar the same solid background as footer -->
    <MudPaper Elevation="1" Class="mb-4"
              Style="background-color:#324755; color:#ffffff;">
        <MudCarousel Class="mud-width-full"
                     Style="height:420px; background-color:#324755;"
                     ShowArrows="@arrows"
                     ShowBullets="@bullets"
                     EnableSwipeGesture="@enableSwipeGesture"
                     AutoCycle="@autocycle"
                     TData="string">

            @foreach (var src in _carousel1)
            {
                <MudCarouselItem>
                    <div class="d-flex" style="height:100%; width:100%; align-items:center; justify-content:center;">
                        <img src="@src"
                             alt="Image"
                             style="max-width:100%; max-height:100%; object-fit:contain;" />
                    </div>
                </MudCarouselItem>
            }
        </MudCarousel>
    </MudPaper>
</MudPaper>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-4">
    This project introduces a Disaster Impact Estimation Prediction (DIEP) framework, a data-driven
    approach that leverages geo-located social media self-reports to estimate disaster impacts in near real 
    time. By combining social media data with official FEMA records and environmental datasets, the 
    framework produces probabilistic predictions of damage and assistance needs across multiple categories 
    at the zip code level.
</MudText>


<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" GutterBottom="true">Conceptual Approach</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" GutterBottom="true">
    The DIEP framework is built on the idea that social media acts as a form of Volunteered Geographic 
    Information (VGI), where individuals report on conditions during disasters through posts, images, and 
    descriptions. These self-reports contain valuable signals about local damage conditions, even though they 
    may be noisy or incomplete. The model is developed using historical tropical storms in Texas, including 
    Hurricane Harvey, Imelda, and Beryl. 
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" GutterBottom="true">
 To extract meaningful insights, the system integrates three primary data sources:
</MudText>
<!-- Do not include bullet points inside MudText could mess up formatting -->
<ul class="mt-2 mb-4" style="margin-left: 2rem;">
    <li class="mb-1">Social Media Data: Over 1 million geo-tagged posts collected during major Texas storm events 
        provide real-time observations of damage and impacts.</li>
    <li class="mb-1">FEMA Disaster Records: Household-level assistance and damage records serve as ground truth 
        for model training and validation.</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Environmental and Climate Data: Precipitation, wind, and other variables provide contextual 
        information about storm intensity and spatial variability.</li>
</ul>

<MudPaper Elevation="1"
          Class="my-6 p-2"
          Style="background-color:#324755; display:flex; justify-content:center; align-items:center;">
    <img src="images/Dataset.png"
         alt="Image"
         style="max-width:100%; height:auto; object-fit:contain;" />
</MudPaper>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-4" GutterBottom="true">
    These datasets are spatially aligned at the zip code level, enabling the model to learn relationships
between observed social media activity and documented disaster impacts.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" GutterBottom="true">Machine Learning Framework</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" GutterBottom="true">
    At the core of the system is a neural network-based prediction model, with a strong emphasis on Natural 
    Language Processing (NLP). Social media text is processed using transformer-based models (specifically 
    BERT), which convert large volumes of unstructured text into structured representations that capture 
    contextual meaning. 
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" GutterBottom="true">
    The model predicts 11 FEMA-related impact variables, including:
</MudText>

<ul class="mt-2" style="margin-left: 2rem;">
    <li class="mb-1">Structural damage (e.g., flood damage, roof damage, destroyed structures).</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Insurance indicators (e.g., flood insurance, homeowner insurance)</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Assistance eligibility (e.g., temporary shelter, repair, rental, replacement assistance)</li>
</ul>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-4" GutterBottom="true">
    Each output is expressed as a probability, indicating the likelihood that a given impact occurred within a 
    specific zip code.
</MudText>

<MudPaper Class="p-4 my-6">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Align="Align.Center" Class="mb-2">Predicted Variables and Model Architecture</MudText>

    <MudPaper Elevation="1" Class="mb-4"
              Style="background-color:#324755; color:#ffffff;">
        <MudCarousel Class="mud-width-full"
                     Style="height:420px; background-color:#324755;"
                     ShowArrows="@arrows"
                     ShowBullets="@bullets"
                     EnableSwipeGesture="@enableSwipeGesture"
                     AutoCycle="@autocycle"
                     TData="string">

            @foreach (var src in _carousel2)
            {
                <MudCarouselItem>
                    <div class="d-flex" style="height:100%; width:100%; align-items:center; justify-content:center;">
                        <img src="@src"
                             alt="Image"
                             style="max-width:100%; max-height:100%; object-fit:contain;" />
                    </div>
                </MudCarouselItem>
            }
        </MudCarousel>
    </MudPaper>
</MudPaper>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" GutterBottom="true">Performance and Capabilities</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" GutterBottom="true">
    The DIEP framework demonstrates strong predictive performance across multiple disaster scenarios:
</MudText>

<ul class="mt-2 mb-4" style="margin-left: 2rem;">
    <li class="mb-1">Achieves high accuracy (up to ~98%) in several key impact categories</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Cross-storm validation successfully demonstrated the model’s generalizability across different 
        storm events, enabling prediction for future disasters</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Processes large datasets efficiently, supporting near real-time applications</li>
</ul>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" GutterBottom="true">Visualization and Decisicion Support</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" GutterBottom="true">
    A key component of this project is the integration of results into interactive geospatial dashboards. These 
    dashboards allow users to:
</MudText>

<ul class="mt-2" style="margin-left: 2rem;">
    <li class="mb-1">Visualize predicted damage probabilities across regions</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Visualize predicted impacts for multiple storms</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Explore spatial patterns of disaster impacts</li>
</ul>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" GutterBottom="true">
    This capability transforms the model into a decision-support tool for emergency managers, planners, and researchers.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-4" GutterBottom="true">
    Click here [embed link] to view the ArcGIS experience dashboard.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" GutterBottom="true">Downscaling and Spatial Refinement</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" GutterBottom="true">
    In addition to zip code-level predictions, the project explores downscaling techniques to improve 
    predictive accuracy and spatial resolution. This enables:
</MudText>

<ul class="mt-2 mb-4" style="margin-left: 2rem;">
    <li class="mb-1">More localized impact assessments</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Enhanced usability for community-level planning</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Integration with finer-scale geographic units such as census block groups</li>
</ul>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" GutterBottom="true">Significance and Applications</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" GutterBottom="true">
    This framework demonstrates how emerging data sources and machine learning can augment traditional
    disaster assessment workflows. Potential applications include:
</MudText>

<ul class="mt-2" style="margin-left: 2rem;">
    <li class="mb-1">Rapid damage estimation for emergency response</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Near real-time visualization of evolving disaster events</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Potential for enhanced multimodal modeling</li>
</ul>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-4" GutterBottom="true">
    By leveraging widely available data and scalable methods, the DIEP framework offers a cost-effective
    and adaptable approach to disaster impact assessment.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" GutterBottom="true">Further Information</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" GutterBottom="true">
    For additional technical details regarding the methodology, data, machine learning framework, and model
    evaluation, please refer to the full research paper available here: (link to paper).
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" GutterBottom="true">
    Datasets used within this project can be found here: (link to GRIIDC)
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.body2" GutterBottom="true">
    The source code, downscaling workflows, and supporting implementation materials for this project are 
    available on GitHub at: (link to GitHub)
</MudText>

</MudContainer>

@code {
    private readonly string[] _carousel1 =
    {
        "images/Key_Stats_Disasters.png",
        "images/Impact_of_Disasters.png",
        "images/Solution_AI_pwrd_dmg_est.png"
    };

    private readonly string[] _carousel2 =
    {
        "images/Target_Variables.png",
        "images/Model_Arch.png",
    };

    private bool arrows = true;
    private bool bullets = true;
    private bool enableSwipeGesture = true;
    private bool autocycle = true;
}